{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>I call witness against you, etc.</b> This verse seems superfluous, for He said nearby (Deuteronomy 30:15): ״רְאֵה נָתַתִּי לְפָנֶיךָ הַיּוֹם אֶת הַחַיִּים וְאֶת הַטּוֹב״ etc. Further, one needs to know why He changed from the order He stated above, making a division of the good by itself, as He said (Deuteronomy 30:15): ״אֶת הַחַיִּים וְאֶת הַטּוֹב, אֶת הַמָּוֶת וְאֶת הָרָע״, whereas here He said: ״הַחַיִּים וְהַמָּוֶת״ etc. ״הַבְּרָכָה וְהַקְּלָלָה״. Further, one needs to know why He said only ״וּבָחַרְתָּ בַּחַיִּים״, and did not say ״וְאֶת הַטּוֹב״." "<b>And it appears</b> upon reflection, why He interposed the phrase ״נָתַתִּי לְפָנֶיךָ״ between the phrase ״הַחַיִּים וְהַמָּוֶת״ and the phrase ״הַבְּרָכָה וְהַקְּלָלָה״ — for He should have either preceded and said ״נָתַתִּי לְפָנֶיךָ הַחַיִּים״ etc., or after saying ״וְהַקְּלָלָה״ then said ״נָתַתִּי לְפָנֶיךָ״?" "<b>Now</b>, the intent of the verse is to make a point: that two destinies — one good and one evil — exist in this world. Therefore He said in this verse: ״הַחַיִּים וְהַמָּוֶת נָתַתִּי לְפָנֶיךָ״ — meaning, reward and punishment that are set before you, that is, in this world, are the life and the death. If they do good — they shall live, and if they sin — they shall die. These two are before you in this world." "<b>And there are</b> two others, namely the blessing and the curse, which are destined for the World to Come, and they are not before him until after death, in the manner of His saying (Deuteronomy 28:6): ״בָּרוּךְ אַתָּה בְּבֹאֶךָ וּבָרוּךְ אַתָּה בְּצֵאתֶךָ״, and conversely אָרוּר הוּא etc., and our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Bava Metzi'a 107a) that it speaks of his departure from the world. And therefore He interposed the phrase ״נָתַתִּי לְפָנֶיךָ״ between the first two destinies and the latter two." "<b>And therefore</b> He concluded by saying ״וּבָחַרְתָּ בַּחַיִּים״ — which is the destiny of this world, for a person's heart is drawn more closely to what is before him, in the manner of His saying (Deuteronomy 11:13): ״וְהָיָה אִם שָׁמֹעַ תִּשְׁמְעוּ״ etc. ״וְנָתַתִּי מְטַר אַרְצְכֶם״ — see that He did not mention inclining the human heart except by means of things revealed in this world." "<b>So too</b> He said here ״וּבָחַרְתָּ בַּחַיִּים״ — meaning, the matter is obligatory even according to physical nature, to choose life. And although in the preceding verse (Deuteronomy 30:15) He said ״רְאֵה נָתַתִּי לְפָנֶיךָ הַחַיִּים וְהַטּוֹב״ together, there the phrase ״לְפָנֶיךָ״ was intended to mean to choose them, whereas here the phrase ״לְפָנֶיךָ״ was intended to mean that which is before you, and He alluded to this intention by placing it in the middle of the promises, as I explained, and also by concluding with ״וּבָחַרְתָּ בַּחַיִּים״, as I explained."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:20:45.873933195Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}